/-! # Homogeneous invertible modules are algebraically trivial

Source: Milne, Abelian Varieties, I §8, Proposition 8.4(b), pp.35–36,
Remark 8.7(c), p.37, and Proposition 8.14 and its following family, pp.39–40.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-abelian-homogeneous-line-algebraic-triviality)
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory MonoidalCategory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- An invertible module on an abelian variety over an algebraically closed field that
is invariant under every rational right translation is algebraically equivalent to the
structure sheaf. Algebraic equivalence is the existing equivalence relation generated by
pairs of fibres of invertible modules over smooth connected parameter varieties.
There is no restriction on the characteristic or the dimension.

Source: Milne, Abelian Varieties, I §8, Proposition 8.4(b), pp.35–36,
Remark 8.7(c), p.37, and Proposition 8.14 and its following family, pp.39–40.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-abelian-homogeneous-line-algebraic-triviality)

Proof:
1. By lem-sc-abelian-variety-projective, choose an ample invertible module M on A.
2. Apply lem-sfb-ample-translation-difference-surjectivity to M and L. The homogeneous
hypothesis gives a rational point a with L isomorphic to t_a^*M tensor M inverse.
This uses the homogeneous definition of Pic^0, without assuming algebraic triviality.
3. On A times A form F = m^*M tensor p_1^*(M inverse). Local bases, their duals and
pulled-back tensor bases show that F is invertible, using def-sc-invertible-module,
def-pc-module-pullback and def-pur-sheaf-tensor-product. For i_b(x) = (x,b), the identities
m i_b = t_b and p_1 i_b = id, together with composition of tensor pullbacks, identify
its fibre with t_b^*M tensor M inverse. Pullback respects tensor products by the local
pure-tensor map b tensor (p tensor q) to (b tensor p) tensor (1 tensor q).
Evaluation therefore identifies the fibre at 0 with O_A and the fibre at a with L.
4. By def-sc-abelian-variety the parameter A is smooth and connected; it is separated
and of finite type because it is proper. The two fibres give a generating pair of
algebraic equivalence (def-sfb-line-algebraic-equivalence). Symmetry gives L equivalent
to O_A. Neither distinct parameter points nor positive dimension is required. -/
theorem IsAbelianVariety.areAlgebraicallyEquivalent_unit_of_homogeneous
    {k : Type u} [Field k] [IsAlgClosed k]
    (A : Over (Spec (.of k))) [GrpObj A] [IsAbelianVariety A]
    (L : A.left.Modules) [Scheme.Modules.IsInvertible L]
    (homogeneous : ∀ a : 𝟙_ (Over (Spec (.of k))) ⟶ A,
      Nonempty ((Scheme.Modules.tensorPullback (GrpObj.mulRight A a).left).obj L ≅ L)) :
    Scheme.Modules.AreAlgebraicallyEquivalent A L
      (SheafOfModules.unit A.left.ringCatSheaf) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
